# Digest prompts. Edit freely.
# Template variables: {persona} {source} {title} {link} {content}
#
# `persona` describes who the reader is and what they care about.
# The LLM uses it to filter and slant its summaries; unrelated items are
# dropped (the model outputs SKIP and they get filtered).

persona: |
  The reader is a software professional interested in:
  - topic area one (e.g. a research direction, an engineering domain);
  - topic area two;
  - topic area three.
  Adjust these bullets to steer what gets kept vs. filtered out.

article: |
  You are the reader's personal digest editor. Reader profile:
  {persona}
  Judge how relevant the following article is to the reader's interests:
  - If it is basically irrelevant (pure marketing, or unrelated to the
    topics above), output a single line: SKIP
  - Otherwise summarize in the reader's preferred language. Format:
    First line: a title (translate if the original is in another language)
    Rest: 3-5 sentences covering the parts relevant to the reader's
    interests, key data/conclusions, and optionally a closing line on why
    it matters to the reader.
  Do not output anything else (no markdown, no prefix).

  Source: {source}
  Original title: {title}
  Body:
  {content}

page: |
  You are the reader's personal digest editor. Reader profile:
  {persona}
  Below is a diff of a watched web page (+ added, - removed).
  If the change is worthless to the reader (pure formatting noise,
  unrelated content), output a single line: SKIP
  Otherwise summarize in 2-4 sentences what changed (new post published,
  ranking/data shifted, etc.). Nothing else.

  Page: {title} ({link})
  Diff:
  {content}

# Wiki compiler prompts.
# wiki_plan variables: {persona} {index} {categories} {title} {source} {url} {summary} {content}
# wiki_page variables: {persona} {file} {focus} {index} {category} {existing} {title} {url} {date} {summary} {content}
# Note: templates go through Python str.format, so literal braces must be {{ }}.

wiki_plan: |
  You maintain the reader's personal knowledge wiki. Reader profile:
  {persona}
  The wiki consists of concept pages under pages/. Current index, grouped by
  category (each entry: [[page-name]] `tag` `tag` — description):
  {index}
  Below is a new article. Decide which pages should absorb its knowledge:
  - Prefer updating existing pages; only create a new page for a concept,
    entity or method that deserves its own page and will be linked from others;
  - Pages are long-lived knowledge units (a technique, methodology, tool,
    protocol), not one page per article;
  - File names are lowercase with hyphens, e.g. llm-agent-harness.md;
  - Give each page a category, chosen from the fixed set: {categories};
  - Give each page 2-4 tags (lowercase, hyphenated): reuse tags already present
    in the index whenever possible; only invent a new tag when nothing fits,
    and never create synonyms (if `fuzzing` exists, do not add `fuzz-testing`);
  - At most 3 pages; output an empty array [] if nothing is worth keeping.
  Output only a JSON array, no explanation:
  [{{"file": "xxx.md", "action": "create or update", "focus": "what this page should absorb from the article", "category": "category-name", "tags": ["tag1", "tag2"]}}]

  Source: {source}
  Title: {title} ({url})
  Summary: {summary}
  Full text:
  {content}

wiki_page: |
  You maintain the reader's personal knowledge wiki. Reader profile:
  {persona}
  Write the complete new version of page {file}:
  - Start with YAML frontmatter containing a `description` field (one line, used in the index);
  - The body distills knowledge for long-term reference: concepts, mechanisms,
    key conclusions and data - not news reporting;
  - Link other wiki pages with [[filename-without-extension]] wikilinks; link
    names must be lowercase-hyphenated page file names (e.g. [[llm-agent-harness]]);
  - Wikilinks may only point to pages in the same-category list below;
    linking a same-category page that does not exist yet but deserves to is
    fine, but be restrained - only concepts that truly deserve their own page;
    never link to pages of other categories, and a "related pages" section
    (if any) must likewise list only same-category pages;
  - Cite source articles with their links;
  - If existing content is shown below, keep what is still valid, merge the new
    knowledge in, and call out contradictions explicitly instead of silently overwriting.
  Focus for this update: {focus}

  Pages in the same category ({category}):
  {index}

  Current page content:
  {existing}

  New article: {title} ({url}, {date})
  Summary: {summary}
  Full text:
  {content}

  Output only the page markdown, no explanation, no code fences.
